    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Randolph-VT/Subject-Matter-Expert--SME----Online-Course-Development--AFF-1015--Meat-Processing-Sanitation-and-HACCP_R-2026328": {
      "title": "Subject Matter Expert (SME) - Online Course Development, AFF-1015: Meat Processing Sanitation and HACCP",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nProgram Overview and Course Structure VTSU CAFFE – The Applied Meat Processing & Butchery Arts Certificate Program is a hybrid training program for a career in the meat industry. The meat industry demands technical expertise with a deep understanding of safety and ethical standards. As consumers increasingly seek transparency and quality, professionals who can merge skill and regulatory knowledge are in high demand. This certificate gives candidates the chance to build a robust foundation while adapting to the ever-changing landscape of food production and processing. To meet these evolving needs, the MeatCraft Butcher Program at VTSU provides a comprehensive curriculum, within three stackable levels, designed to develop well-rounded professionals Total Credits Level 1: MeatCraft Apprentice Butcher:   13 credits Level 2: MeatCraft Artisan Butcher:         23 credits Level 3: MeatCraft Master Butcher:         30 credits Program Outcomes Understand the science and develop skills to expertly process beef, pork, lamb and poultry. Employ industry standards of safety for meat products and meat fabrication. Tackle realistic problems and variables in complex, high-pressure workforce scenarios. Demonstrate value-added skills with sausage-making, marketing, and culinary techniques (Level 2). Understand advanced meat product development, and be able to employ ethical and humane methods for livestock harvesting (Level 3). Complete a hands-on abattoir internship within a production environment (Level 3). Identify career pathways in the industry and establish professional contacts. Program Structure: Description and Details Structure Sequential, skill-building format, practical understanding, foundational concepts, specialized techniques, safety, sanitation, butchery, processing, regulatory compliance Format Hybrid delivery, online lecture, hands-on intensives, mentorship, peer collaboration, focused instruction, abattoir (meat facility) internship, regional partner sites, traceability, humane handling, value-added meat products Completion Certificate in Meat Processing and Food Safety, Credentials valued by employers Targeted Population High school students (dual and early college enrollment), working adults, culinary professionals, aspiring butchers, Retail meat operations, processing plants, butcher shops, local food systems As an SME, you will create and revise original course materials following VTSU Online’s established course development process, including templates, milestones, and quality reviews. Compensation per course is $ 1,000.00/credit. Course Details: AFF-1015: Meat Processing Sanitation and HACCP Catalog Title: Meat Processing Sanitation and HACCP Credit Hours: 2 Format: 100% Online, AFF1015 Catalog Description: This course covers safe and sanitary practices in meat processing environments. Students learn about microbial hazards, effective sanitation methods, and the principles of HACCP (Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point), a systematic approach to food safety required in all USDA-inspected meat processing plants. This course highlights the crucial role of sanitation in protecting public health and maintaining product quality, and prepares students to sit for the HACCP exam. Learning Outcomes: Identify biological, chemical, and physical hazards in meat processing environments. Implement cleaning and sanitation protocols for equipment and workspaces. Adopt personal hygiene and facility design principles to minimize risks. Apply federal regulations related to sanitation and inspection. Explain the seven principles of HACCP and their role in food safety systems. Develop a compliant HACCP plan tailored to meat products.   Prerequisites: None. Minimum Qualifications Required minimum qualifications: Qualified candidates should have applied (workforce) subject matter experience, either through direct work experience in the industry, educational degree(s)/certifications, or a combination thereof.  Prior experience teaching is preferred.  Applicants should include a brief statement of interest and a resume or CV showing any relevant work and/or educational experience.  Scheduled Hours Weekly: 40 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $1,000/credit Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: T02 Job Posting Responsibilities:"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Randolph-VT/Subject-Matter-Expert--SME--Opportunity--Online-Course-Development--AFF-1030--Introduction-to-Regenerative-Grazing_R-2026329-1": {
      "title": "Subject Matter Expert (SME) Opportunity: Online Course Development, AFF-1030: Introduction to Regenerative Grazing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nProgram Overview and Course Structure The Regenerative Grazing and Livestock Systems Certificate Program prepares you for a fulfilling career in the livestock farming industry. In this hybrid, 24-credit accredited program, you will develop the well-rounded skills to manage ruminant (beef and dairy cattle, sheep and goat) grazing, as the cornerstone of a modern and profitable agricultural operation. Through coursework and a capstone internship experience you will be connected to leaders in the agricultural practices of adaptive multi-paddock grazing, and develop a host of unique skills to put regenerative grazing into practice. Areas of study include: fence and water system design, sound pasture plant management, soil health management, selection criteria for livestock genetics, development of comprehensive grazing management plans, and, the right business and problem-solving skills to run a successful and modern livestock business. Program Outcomes Understand regenerative livestock production systems. Understand and design forage and pasture management systems, taking into full account the variables of soil, forage plants, cover crops, rotational density and herd. Identify key components of ruminant nutrition, infectious diseases, pests, and herd genetics/breeding, and understand the role of pasture management within the context of herd health. Effectively use farm management tools and business knowledge to create a financially sustainable grazing business model. Design, construct and maintain fencing and watering systems. Tackle realistic problems and variables in complex, high-pressure workforce scenarios. Create an implementable custom grazing management plan for an actual farm. Identify career pathways in the industry and establish professional contacts. As an SME, you will create and revise original course materials following VTSU Online’s established course development process, including templates, milestones, and quality reviews. Compensation per course is $1,000.00/credit. Course Details: AFF-1030: Introduction to Regenerative Grazing Credit Hours: 1 Format: 100% Online, AFF1030 Catalog Description: This course serves as an introduction to regenerative grazing practices and best practice. Students will apply the status quo approach to agriculture and livestock management in the U.S., including crop monocultures and confined animal feed operations (CAFOs). Students will become proficient in identification of alternatives to conventional systems by understanding key principles of natural systems and how they inform regenerative grazing approaches. Learning outcomes: Understand livestock production systems. Define regenerative grazing and understand the relationship to natural systems. Explain how regenerative grazing differs from conventional livestock production systems. Describe the One Health concerns with intensive agricultural production. Describe how regenerative grazing can support human-environmental health.   Prerequisites: None. Minimum Qualifications Required minimum qualifications: Qualified candidates should have applied (workforce) subject matter experience, either through direct work experience in the industry, educational degree(s)/certifications, or a combination thereof.  Prior experience teaching is preferred.  Applicants should include a brief statement of interest and a resume or CV showing any relevant work and/or educational experience.  Scheduled Hours Weekly: 40 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $1,000.00/credit. Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: T02 Job Posting Responsibilities:"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Randolph-VT/Subject-Matter-Expert--SME--Opportunity--Online-Course-Development--AFF-1065--Applied-Communication---Problem-Solving_R-2026331": {
      "title": "Subject Matter Expert (SME) Opportunity: Online Course Development, AFF-1065: Applied Communication & Problem Solving",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nProgram Overview and Course Structure VTSU CAFFE – The Applied Meat Processing & Butchery Arts Certificate Program is a hybrid training program for a career in the meat industry. The meat industry demands technical expertise with a deep understanding of safety and ethical standards. As consumers increasingly seek transparency and quality, professionals who can merge skill and regulatory knowledge are in high demand. This certificate gives candidates the chance to build a robust foundation while adapting to the ever-changing landscape of food production and processing. To meet these evolving needs, the MeatCraft Butcher Program at VTSU provides a comprehensive curriculum, within three stackable levels, designed to develop well-rounded professionals As an SME, you will create and revise original course materials following VTSU Online’s established course development process, including templates, milestones, and quality reviews. Compensation per course is $1,000.00/credit. Course Details: AFF-1065: Applied Communication & Problem Solving Catalog Title: Applied Communication & Problem Solving Credit Hours: 3 Format: 100% Online, AFF1065 Catalog Description: Development of written, verbal and non-verbal communication skills through effective use of logic. Practical skills in critical thinking, problem-solving, teamwork, and communication will be cultivated through an engagement in scenario-based explorations of a fictional zombie apocalypse. Emphasis is placed on collaborative decision-making, stress resilience, and real-time reasoning; preparing students to perform with clarity and confidence in high-stakes workforce environments. Learning Objectives: Upon successful completion of this course, students will be able to: Tackle realistic problems and variables in complex, high-pressure workforce scenarios. Communicate clearly and persuasively in both written and oral formats. Hone ability to form rational justifications to affirm decisions made during simulations and discussions. Apply adaptive thinking and resourcefulness to navigate unpredictable challenges. Work cooperatively under stress, managing group dynamics and problem-solving in urgent, evolving situations. Develop strategies that build on personal strengths and challenges related to decision-making under stress. Engage in a challenging and creative learning environment (while hopefully avoiding being eaten).   Prerequisite: No prior survival training required—just an open mind and a sense of humor. Minimum Qualifications Required minimum qualifications: Qualified candidates should have applied (workforce) subject matter experience, either through direct work experience in the industry, educational degree(s)/certifications, or a combination thereof.  Prior experience teaching is preferred.  Applicants should include a brief statement of interest and a resume or CV showing any relevant work and/or educational experience.  Scheduled Hours Weekly: 40 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $1,000.00/credit. Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: T02 Job Posting Responsibilities:"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Johnson-VT/Student-Development-Counselor-Upward-Bound_R-2026374-1": {
      "title": "Student Development Counselor Upward Bound",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBASIC FUNCTION GRADE To coordinate or provide tutoring, counseling, and academic support services for Upward Bound students during academic year and summer programs. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES Coordinate and/or provide individual tutoring and counseling to eligible students regularly during the academic year and as a daily part of summer programs. Assess students' academic, personal and life skills through regular high school and home visits to determine areas of need; maintain regular communication between the home, school, and Upward Bound Office; develop, maintain and monitor personal learning plans with eligible students. Provide college selection and financial aid counseling for eligible high school seniors including, visiting colleges and college fairs, assisting with applications and counseling on financial aid matters. Assist with the development and implementation of career exploration for eligible students, including inventories, values clarification, résumé writing and interests matching for summer internships and future college majors/minors. Assist with open houses, parent nights and other target school events at participating schools. Coordinate a variety of student activities, including community service projects, college fairs, college visits, student leadership conferences, and other student development conferences or activities. Assist with planning of Saturday Sessions, including providing workshops and student activities to enhance student development. Develop and maintain resource lists for social and educational services for resource and referral information. Assist with recruitment of students, eligibility assessment, program planning efforts and preparation and implementation of the summer program. Provide residential counseling and supports to students and staff during the six week campus academic program. Assist in carrying out post-secondary placement of students and provide assistance to students and families throughout the college application and financial aid application process. Demonstrate inclusivity and provide excellent customer service in all modalities, including in-person, telephone, e-mail, video call, live chat, texting, and the like. Compile and maintain appropriate records on program participants and activities, as well as perform routine administrative tasks and prepare reports related to grant funding. Confer regularly with Upward Bound and other University staff and community service providers. Attend relevant local, regional and national professional meetings. Perform related tasks as assigned. Upward Bound Student Development Counselor: SUPERVISION RECEIVED Direct supervision is received from the Director of Upward Bound. SUPERVISION EXERCISED Partial administrative and functional supervision of summer student workers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelors degree in education, behavioral sciences or a related field or other appropriate discipline, with master's degree desirable, plus 2 to 4 years of relevant counseling or teaching experience, or a combination of education and experience from which comparable knowledge and skills are acquired. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES Knowledge: Strong base of general knowledge and skills relevant to the program, such as counseling, working with community action agencies, educational program design, understanding of post-secondary opportunities and admissions policies/processes, and the like. Experience working in a high school setting or in providing basic personal and academic counseling. Knowledge of community resources and agencies related to a variety of student needs. • Familiarity with post-secondary and financial aid application processes and college readiness activities. Understanding of the psychological and cultural characteristics of economically and educationally disadvantaged students, particularly in a rural setting. Skills: Excellent counseling skills. Good planning, administrative, organizational, and supervisory skills. Excellent interpersonal and oral and written communication skills. Proven record of accountability and follow-through with the ability to meet deadlines. Abilities: Ability to deal effectively with and serve as a program representative to students, parents, secondary and post-secondary school personnel, community agencies, funding agencies, and the like, as well as various College personnel, requiring skills in individual counseling, coordinating and public presentation. Demonstrated positive attitude regarding Vermont State University and a desire to improve student outcomes. Demonstrated ability to support a diverse community and promote diverse perspectives and cultures. Scheduled Hours Weekly: 37.5 Budgeted Wage for this Position: 44,000-50,000 Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: S11 Job Posting Responsibilities: Unit Determination: Professional, Administrative, Technical - VSC United Professionals"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Castleton-VT/Lead-University-Counselor_R-2026404-1": {
      "title": "Lead University Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBASIC FUNCTION The Lead University Counselor provides advanced clinical counseling services and serves as the lead clinician for Vermont State University's counseling program. In addition to providing comprehensive mental health assessment, crisis intervention, treatment planning, and short-term counseling services, this position provides clinical supervision and functional leadership to University Counselors, counseling interns, practicum students, and student employees. The Lead University Counselor coordinates the daily clinical operations of counseling services, serves as the primary clinical resource for complex student mental health concerns, and supports the Director of Health and Wellness in implementing counseling initiatives, clinical best practices, and quality improvement efforts. ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIE S Plan, implement, coordinate, monitor, and evaluate the University's counseling services as part of the Health and Wellness Department. Provide comprehensive mental health assessment, diagnosis, treatment planning, crisis intervention, and short-term counseling services to students. Provide clinical supervision, mentoring, and work direction to University Counselors as assigned, graduate counseling interns, practicum students, undergraduate interns, and student employees. Coordinate daily counseling schedules and clinical coverage to ensure continuity of counseling services. Serve as the primary clinical consultation resource for counselors regarding complex student mental health concerns, crisis response, risk assessment, and treatment planning. Review clinical documentation and provide guidance regarding counseling best practices, ethical standards, and applicable licensing requirements. Assist the Director of Health and Wellness with implementing counseling procedures, evidence-based clinical practices, and continuous quality improvement initiatives. Provide short-term counseling, psychological assessment, crisis intervention, and referral to campus or community resources as clinically appropriate. Serve as a consultant and trainer for Residential Life, Student Success, CARE Teams, faculty, and staff regarding student mental health concerns, crisis response, and behavioral intervention. Instruct, advise, and provide educational presentations regarding mental health, resilience, student well-being, and related counseling topics. Confer regularly with Student Success staff, faculty, parents, community mental health providers, and other University personnel regarding clinical consultation and coordination of student support. Participate in multidisciplinary case consultation meetings involving students with complex mental health concerns. Collect and analyze counseling utilization and outcome data and provide recommendations to the Director to support continuous improvement of counseling services. Plan and administer counseling record systems and ensure compliance with professional documentation standards. Keep abreast of developments in clinical counseling, higher education mental health, and evidence-based treatment practices. Participate on University committees and multidisciplinary teams as assigned. Perform other related duties as assigned.   SUPERVISION RECEIVED Direct supervision is received from the Director of Health and Wellness.   SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITY Direct supervision to University Counselors, graduate counseling interns, practicum students, undergraduate interns, and student employees. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Masters degree in counseling or related field with appropriate license or certification, plus three (3) to five (5) years of progressively responsible clinical counseling experience, including crisis intervention and behavioral health assessment and a minimum of  two (2) experience supervising or mentoring professional staff, graduate interns, or practicum students, or a combination of education and experience from which comparable knowledge and skills are acquired. Valid driver's license and willingness to obtain a VSC fleet license. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES Advanced knowledge of clinical mental health counseling principles, assessment, diagnosis, crisis intervention, treatment planning, and evidence-based therapeutic practices. Knowledge of college student development, behavioral health, trauma-informed care, and emerging mental health issues affecting higher education populations. Knowledge of applicable federal and state laws, ethical standards, and professional licensing requirements, including FERPA, HIPAA (as applicable), Title IX, and mandated reporting obligations. Knowledge of clinical supervision principles, professional development practices, and mentoring techniques. Understanding of higher education systems, student success initiatives, CARE/Behavioral Intervention Team processes, and interdisciplinary student support models. Knowledge of electronic health record systems, clinical documentation standards, and quality assurance practices. Knowledge of diversity, equity, inclusion, accessibility, and culturally responsive counseling practices. Skills Advanced clinical assessment, diagnosis, crisis intervention, and short-term counseling skills. Clinical supervision, coaching, mentoring, and staff development skills. Leadership and coordination skills with the ability to provide functional leadership without full administrative authority. Strong interpersonal, consultation, and relationship-building skills. Excellent verbal, written, and presentation communication skills. Conflict resolution, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills. Strong organizational and time management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities. Proficiency using electronic health record systems, Microsoft Office, and other technologies supporting counseling services. Ability to analyze clinical issues, identify trends, and recommend improvements to counseling practices. Abilities Ability to lead and coordinate the daily clinical operations of counseling services while fostering collaboration and teamwork. Ability to supervise, mentor, and evaluate counselors, interns, and student employees while promoting professional growth and accountability. Ability to provide sound clinical judgment in complex, high-risk, and crisis situations. Ability to consult effectively with faculty, staff, students, families, and community providers while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. Ability to build trust and establish effective working relationships with diverse individuals and multidisciplinary teams. Ability to interpret and apply professional standards, institutional policies, and legal requirements while exercising independent judgment. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality and exercise discretion in handling sensitive information. Ability to promote an inclusive, equitable, and student-centered environment that supports the success and well-being of all students. Ability to adapt to changing priorities, lead through change, and respond effectively during emergencies. Ability to represent the counseling function professionally and positively throughout the University. Scheduled Hours Weekly: 37.5 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $70,000 - $73,000 Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: S14 Job Posting Responsibilities: Unit Determination: Supervisors - VSC United Professionals"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Castleton-VT/Graduate-Assistant-Coach--Men-s-Hockey-_R-2026444-1": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant Coach (Men's Hockey)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nVermont State University is seeking qualified candidates for Graduate Assistant Coach positions in Athletics. Graduate Assistant Coaches play an important role in supporting the success, development, and experience of our student athletes both on and off the field. Responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Assisting with the coordination and supervision of practices and contests;   Supporting recruitment activities for prospective student athletes; Coordinating team travel and meals; Assisting with eligibility procedures and compliance requirements; Supporting athletic communications and sports information functions; Performing additional duties as assigned. Graduate Assistant Coaches will report directly to the Head Coach of the assigned sport. Graduate Assistant Coach appointments are typically structured as two one-year appointments, contingent upon satisfactory academic progress, job performance, program needs, and approval by the University. Graduate Assistant coach positions include tuition remission for up to 18 graduate credits; on-campus housing and a $5,000 stipend. Under current IRS regulations, graduate tuition benefits exceeding the annual non-taxable threshold of $5,250 will be treated as taxable income and subject to applicable withholding taxes. Any resulting tax liability is the responsibility of the graduate assistant. Graduate Assistants are expected to maintain a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.0 and as representatives of Vermont State University, are expected to demonstrate professionalism, integrity, and good sportsmanship, while fostering those same expectations among student athletes. Adherence to all NCAA, conference, and university regulations is required. Appointment to a Graduate Assistant Coach position may be terminated in cases involving deliberate or serious violations of NCAA regulations or university policies. Scheduled Hours Weekly: 20 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $5k Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: STS Job Posting Responsibilities:"
    },
    "https://vsc.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Johnson-VT/Assistant-Mens-Soccer-Coach_R-2026427": {
      "title": "Assistant Mens Soccer Coach",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nCHARACTERISTIC DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES To assist the Head Coach in coaching the varsity sport of soccer at the University under the guidelines established by the NCAA and NAC including: recruiting student athletes, establishing practice schedules and holding practice sessions, scouting of opponents games, preparing for and coaching during games. Assist in the planning of the overall administration, development, and implementation of varsity athletic programs. Assist to hire, train, and supervise student assistants to carry out tasks related to specific areas such as the fitness center, the pool, and the rock-climbing wall, equipment maintenance, club and intramural programs. Monitor recreation and athletic activities, facilities and equipment on a regular basis, ensuring adherence to established operating procedures, with a focus on safety procedures. Assist in game management for various athletic events and contests. Perform other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED General supervision received from the Head Coach. SUPERVISION EXERCISED General supervision over student workers and student interns. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in sports administration, recreation, physical education, or other appropriate discipline with a master’s degree desirable, plus two to four years relevant experience in recreation/athletics/coaching, or a combination of education and experience from which comparable knowledge and skills are acquired. Strong and varied technical skills and interest in recreational activities. Broad base of knowledge and skills related to fitness center, pool and athletic facilities, management, coaching, recruiting and other relevant experience. Coaching experience. Ability to deal effectively with a wide range of University students, personnel, as well as media contacts, facilities users and others outside the University. Weekend and evening work is required. University Fleet License is required. Scheduled Hours Weekly: 15 Budgeted Wage for this Position: $2,000 Exempt Status: Exempt Grade: T02 Job Posting Responsibilities:"
    },
